<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Lament · 1965 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hasta Siempre, Comandante</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Cuban song of 1965 saying farewell to Che Guevara, a leader of the Cuban Revolution, when he left Cuba.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Carlos Puebla</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Carlos Puebla</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Cuba after the Revolution; the Latin American and European left</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cuba</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1965</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music by Carlos Puebla, 1965)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasta-siempre-comandante">Hasta Siempre, Comandante</a>&quot; (&quot;Until Forever, Commander&quot;), often simply &quot;Hasta Siempre&quot;, was written in 1965 by the Cuban composer Carlos Puebla. Its words answer the farewell letter that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a>, a commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a>, left when he departed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to spread revolution in the Congo and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, where he was captured and killed. The song recalls moments of the Cuban Revolution and presents Guevara and his part in it in admiring terms; its title takes part of Guevara's well-known motto &quot;¡Hasta la victoria siempre!&quot; (&quot;Until victory, always!&quot;). In the manner of Cuban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> song, it has a refrain and five quatrains of eight-syllable lines rhyming ABBA.</p><p>The song became famous after Guevara's death, when many left-leaning artists recorded their own versions; more than two hundred are known, among them Compay Segundo, Soledad Bravo, Silvio Rodríguez, Robert Wyatt, Wolf Biermann and Maria Farantouri. Some are often credited to the wrong singers: Puebla's own recording is sometimes attributed to Víctor Jara, who never sang it. The most commercially successful version, by the French singer Nathalie Cardone, produced by Laurent Boutonnat, reached number two in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and spent 38 weeks on the French charts; the Argentine model Inés Rivero released a rival version at the same time.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of farewell and homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a>; the song presents him and his role as a revolutionary commander with admiration. Its mood is solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="291836" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="10722782" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Carlos-puebla-y-sus-tradicionales-hasta-siempre-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-carlos-puebla-hasta-siempre-commandante-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
